    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d140">

                <pb facs="0162" n="148" sameAs="frus:frus1943v06/pg148"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.711/120</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Cuba</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Braden</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 4898</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Habana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-10-25">October 25, 1943</date>.<lb/>[Received November
                        2.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the
                    Department’s airgram no. A–2323 of October 6, 1943,<anchor n="39"
                        xml:id="d140-f1"/> concerning the submission of statements from the
                    Military, Naval and Legal Attachés and the Cable Censorship Liaison Officer, to
                    constitute positive justification before the Bureau of the Budget for an
                    allocation of funds assisting the inauguration and operation of cable, telegraph
                    and radio censorship in Cuba. The statements requested are enclosed
                        herewith.<anchor n="40" xml:id="d140-f2"/></p>
                <p>I can not fail to take this further opportunity of stressing the importance of
                    closing this gap in the censorship of communications which, as may be seen from
                    the enclosures, is necessary both from the negative and positive sides of
                    censorship. I recommend also that the earliest possible action be taken to make
                    the requisite funds available.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Spruille Braden</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
